  "RateLimitPolicy": {
    "vendorDocumented": false,
    "statement": "🚩 NOT VENDOR-DOCUMENTED for wp/v2 or wc/v3. WordPress core imposes NO rate limiting, and the WooCommerce v3 docs contain no rate-limit section. Verified: 0 occurrences of `RateLimit` anywhere in the derived in-scope spec, and a live GET /wp/v2/posts returned no RateLimit-* headers.",
    "numbersDeliberatelyOmitted": "NO NUMBER IS RECORDED, in this blob or in BatchMaxRequestCount / BatchRequestWaitTime. Emitting one would fabricate a vendor commitment that does not exist.",
    "realWorld": "Actual limits are HOST-IMPOSED and invisible to the vendor contract: managed WordPress hosts, CDNs/WAFs (Cloudflare, Sucuri), mod_security, PHP max_execution_time, and per-site security plugins. They vary per tenant and can change without notice.",
    "connectorObligation": "Honour HTTP 429 + Retry-After ADAPTIVELY; also back off on 503 and on connection resets. Use a conservative default concurrency, because the thing being loaded is the TENANT''S OWN SITE.",
    "theOneException": "The ONLY WordPress-ecosystem API with documented RateLimit-* headers is wc/store/v1 (the Store API), which is OUT OF SCOPE. And even there the defaults are OFF: src/StoreApi/Utilities/RateLimits.php has ENABLED = false, LIMIT = 25, SECONDS = 10, PROXY_SUPPORT = false — the headers appear only on sites that opted in."
  },
  "ErrorResponseShape": {
    "shape": "{ \"code\": \"<machine_code>\", \"message\": \"<human text>\", \"data\": { \"status\": <httpStatus>, ... } }",
    "note": "The standard WP_Error JSON serialisation used by WP_REST_Server for every error across wp/v2, wc/v3 and /batch/v1. `code` is the stable machine-readable discriminator (e.g. rest_forbidden, rest_no_route, woocommerce_rest_authentication_error, woocommerce_rest_request_entity_too_large); `message` is localised and MUST NOT be parsed. Batch sub-request failures are reported per-item inside the batch response envelope, not as a top-level error.",
    "evidenceStrength": "ImpliedFromExample — the shape is consistent across every observed error and across the named error codes, but no single pinned document states it as a contract."
  },
  "CustomObjectMarkerPattern": null,
  "CustomObjectMarkerNote": "🚩 WordPress has NO NAME-MARKER convention for custom objects — nothing analogous to HubSpot''s `p_<accountID>_<name>`. A site-registered custom post type or custom taxonomy flows through the SAME core controllers as a built-in one, with only a different `rest_base`, and appears in the route index indistinguishable from core. Consequence: custom objects can be DISCOVERED (they are in the route index) but cannot be IDENTIFIED as custom by pattern-matching a name. The only reliable discriminator is set subtraction against the declared stock-install floor recorded in DeclaredAgainst. NULL here is correct, not missing.",
  "CustomFieldMarkerPattern": null,
  "CustomFieldMarkerNote": "Same story at the field level. Registered `meta` is a nested object whose keys are PER-SITE — only meta registered with show_in_rest appears — so the field universe is per-site too, independently of the per-site OBJECT universe. There is no marker pattern; per-site meta keys are discovered from OPTIONS, never inferred.",
  "WriteCapability": "read-write",
  "WriteCapabilityNote": "Deliberately NOT declared bidirectional. ''Bidirectional'' would claim two-way SYNC semantics, and WordPress provides none of the machinery that requires: there is no change-log or deleted-records feed (hard deletes leave no tombstone), there are no ETags/If-Match concurrency tokens on the in-scope routes, wp/v2/users and wc/v3/customers expose no modified timestamp at all so their remote state cannot even be diffed, and offset pagination cannot guarantee a complete scan. Reads and writes are both fully supported, but the vendor gives no conflict-resolution or change-capture primitive to build a safe two-way loop on. read-write is the honest ceiling.",
  "WriteCapabilityBindingNote": "BINDING under the floor rule capability-dishonest. The connector may declare and implement create/update/delete. It may NOT declare bidirectional sync, and it must NOT claim delete DETECTION (only a declared key-sweep).",
  "WriteShape": "Core: POST /wp/v2/<collection> (create); POST or PUT/PATCH /wp/v2/<collection>/<id> (update); DELETE /wp/v2/<collection>/<id> (soft-delete to trash for post types, ?force=true for hard delete). Woo wc/v3: the same, plus per-resource batch. Ordinary JSON request bodies; readOnly properties are marked in each get_item_schema().",
  "ConcurrencyControl": "none",
  "ConcurrencyControlNote": "PROVEN ABSENT for the in-scope surface: 0 occurrences of If-Match, If-Unmodified-Since or ETag anywhere in the derived in-scope spec, and no request parameter in wp/v2 or wc/v3 accepts a client-supplied version token. 🚩 One evidenced caveat that is NOT a concurrency primitive: Woo 11.0.1 contains an ETag-GENERATION hook — `get_data_for_etag()` overridden in includes/rest-api/Controllers/Version2/class-wc-rest-products-v2-controller.php:104 (it strips the randomly-sampled `related_ids` before hashing) — and that method is the ONLY ETag reference in the entire includes/rest-api tree; the base class that CALLS it lies outside the acquired file set. That is response-CACHING machinery, not an optimistic-concurrency token a client can send back, so it is unusable as a conflict primitive at these pins and ConcurrencyControl stays `none`.",
  "DeleteSemantics": "mixed",
  "DeleteSemanticsDetail": "SOFT for post types (DELETE moves to `status=trash`, and trashed records remain listable via status=trash). HARD for everything else, and hard for post types when `?force=true` is passed — a hard delete leaves NO tombstone. Orphan deletion is therefore NOT safe to infer from absence; it requires the declared key sweep. See DeclaredCapabilityLimits.no-deleted-records-feed.",
  "DefaultSyncDirection": "Pull",
  "DefaultSyncDirectionNote": "READ-ONLY BY DEFAULT for every object, per the read-first / bidirectional-opt-in rule. Writes are fully supported by the vendor and may be implemented, but push/create/update/delete against a client''s real site must be validated against a sandbox FIRST. Preconditions before bidirectional could ever be enabled for this vendor: (1) a change-capture primitive for the families that have none (wp/v2/users, wc/v3/customers) — there is none today; (2) a deletion signal — there is none, only a key sweep; (3) a concurrency token to make conflict resolution safe — there is none. All three are absent, so bidirectional is not merely off-by-default here, it is not currently justifiable.",
  "DefaultConflictResolution": "Manual",
  "DefaultConflictResolutionReason": "ConcurrencyControl is `none`, so `MostRecent` MUST NOT be recommended — there is no ETag or version token, and two of the in-scope families (wp/v2/users, wc/v3/customers) expose no modified timestamp at all, so their remote state cannot even be diffed to decide which side is newer. With no vendor primitive to arbitrate, `Manual` is the only honest default.",
  "DiscoveryIsAuthoritative": false,
  "DiscoveryIsAuthoritativeRationale": "The two levels differ and ONE BOOLEAN CANNOT EXPRESS BOTH, so DiscoveryIsAuthoritative stays FALSE — the safe default — and the nuance lives here. Field-level deactivation is flatly unsafe (a thin context=view schema would delete real fields). Object-level deactivation is ALSO unsafe once the gating above is admitted: a namespace can vanish from one site''s index because of a feature flag or a lazy-load filter while remaining a real part of the vendor''s surface. Record the object-level authority as an OBSERVATION, never as a deactivation licence.",
  "DiscoveryAuthoritativeness": {
    "objectLevel": "complete-for-enabled-surface-not-for-software",
    "objectLevelNote": "GET {base}/wp-json enumerates every route the site has REGISTERED for that request, and route REGISTRATION is not capability-gated — an under-privileged credential still sees the full route list (only the responses 401). That makes the route index an authoritative enumeration of what THAT CONNECTION can ever address. It is NOT an enumeration of what the SOFTWARE can expose.",
    "objectLevelRejectedValue": "complete-route-index",
    "objectLevelRejectionReason": "DEVIATION D1. The plan''s literal value overstates the guarantee and is disproven three ways at the pinned versions (see objectLevelCaveats). A later reader must not treat ''complete-route-index'' as licence to deactivate objects that a feature flag or a lazy-load filter merely hid on one site at one moment.",
    "objectLevelCaveats": [
      {
        "id": "woo-lazy-loads-namespaces",
        "detail": "WooCommerce LAZY-LOADS namespaces per request via wc_rest_should_load_namespace() (includes/wc-rest-functions.php, since Woo 9.2) over a known_namespaces list of wc/v1, wc/v2, wc/v3, wc/v4, wc-telemetry, wc-admin, wc-analytics, wc/store, wc/private — and the decision is exposed through the public `wc_rest_should_load_namespace` filter, so ANY site can override which namespaces appear."
      },
      {
        "id": "feature-flagged-namespaces",
        "detail": "Whole namespaces sit behind FEATURE FLAGS and are therefore absent from a stock route index while being fully present in the shipped software: wc/v4 requires Features::is_enabled(''rest-api-v4''), which defaults FALSE (includes/rest-api/Server.php:83; includes/react-admin/feature-config.php:42 ''rest-api-v4'' => false), and wc/v4/graphql requires the `dual_code_graphql_api` feature plus PHP 8.1+. Both ship in Woo 11.0.1 and both were verified absent from a stock index (0 live wc/v4 routes)."
      },
      {
        "id": "batch-route-absent-from-namespaces-array",
        "detail": "/batch/v1 is a registered ROUTE (wp-includes/rest-api/class-wp-rest-server.php:115) that never appears in the route index''s `namespaces[]` array at all — the reference instance reports its namespace as EMPTY. A namespaces[]-only enumeration silently misses it."
      }
    ],
    "fieldLevel": "incomplete",
    "fieldLevelNote": "OPTIONS <route> returns a real JSON Schema, but field visibility IS capability-gated (context=edit plus the corresponding capability). An under-privileged credential legitimately receives a THINNER schema than the docs describe, so field ABSENCE at runtime proves NOTHING about the vendor.",
    "deactivationPermitted": false,
    "deactivationPermittedWhy": "The two levels differ and ONE BOOLEAN CANNOT EXPRESS BOTH, so DiscoveryIsAuthoritative stays FALSE — the safe default — and the nuance lives here. Field-level deactivation is flatly unsafe (a thin context=view schema would delete real fields). Object-level deactivation is ALSO unsafe once the gating above is admitted: a namespace can vanish from one site''s index because of a feature flag or a lazy-load filter while remaining a real part of the vendor''s surface. Record the object-level authority as an OBSERVATION, never as a deactivation licence."
  },
